WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:02.100
-Our next guest is an author

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:03.601
and staff writer
at "The Atlantic."

00:00:03.601 --> 00:00:05.801
His new book,
"Romney: A Reckoning,"
is on sale now.

00:00:05.801 --> 00:00:08.934
Please welcome back to the show
McKay Coppins, everybody.

00:00:08.934 --> 00:00:10.667
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:10.667 --> 00:00:16.033
♪♪

00:00:16.033 --> 00:00:21.400
♪♪

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:23.501
Welcome back to the show.
-Thanks for having me.

00:00:23.501 --> 00:00:26.267
-So, this is
a very impressive book

00:00:26.267 --> 00:00:30.300
because of the amount of access
you were granted by Mitt Romney.

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:32.634
He gave you journals,
he gave you texts,

00:00:32.634 --> 00:00:36.167
he gave you access
to e-mails he wrote.

00:00:36.167 --> 00:00:39.400
Are you surprised
at how much he gave you?

00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:42.334
-I was surprised when
he agreed to do it at all.

00:00:42.334 --> 00:00:45.234
-Yeah.
-Because, you know, Mitt Romney,

00:00:45.234 --> 00:00:47.467
was known for most
of his political career

00:00:47.467 --> 00:00:50.167
as, like, this very cautious,

00:00:50.167 --> 00:00:52.834
you know, restrained,
calculated politician

00:00:52.834 --> 00:00:54.634
who was stuck
to his talking points.

00:00:54.634 --> 00:00:56.801
And when I approached him
about doing this book,

00:00:56.801 --> 00:00:58.467
I told him,
"I only want to do this

00:00:58.467 --> 00:01:01.000
if you're ready
to be fully candid."

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:03.801
And I knew he was on board

00:01:03.801 --> 00:01:05.334
when like a couple weeks
into it,

00:01:05.334 --> 00:01:06.567
he sent me a text.

00:01:06.567 --> 00:01:09.234
I was actually at church.
I got a text from him.

00:01:09.234 --> 00:01:10.767
He says,
"Hey. Check your e-mail.

00:01:10.767 --> 00:01:12.567
I sent you something
that might be interesting."

00:01:12.567 --> 00:01:15.334
And it was just hundreds
of pages of his journals.

00:01:15.334 --> 00:01:16.767
And I was like, "Oh, wow.

00:01:16.767 --> 00:01:18.601
Alright.
We're gonna do this for real."

00:01:18.601 --> 00:01:20.467
-He already was --

00:01:20.467 --> 00:01:23.367
He sort of started
as an outsider in the Senate.

00:01:23.367 --> 00:01:27.200
You know, he had been vocally
opposed to Donald Trump,

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:28.934
especially on character.

00:01:28.934 --> 00:01:30.968
And then he joins the Senate,

00:01:30.968 --> 00:01:33.701
where the GOP caucus
is obviously very full-throated

00:01:33.701 --> 00:01:35.200
in their defense
of Donald Trump.

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:37.567
Yet, I imagine he has to be
even more on the outside

00:01:37.567 --> 00:01:39.300
now that this book has come out.

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:41.400
Have you spoken to him
since it's been released?

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:43.334
-Yeah, well, a few weeks ago,

00:01:43.334 --> 00:01:45.868
like, the first excerpts
from the book came out,

00:01:45.868 --> 00:01:49.534
and, you know, he says
some very damning things

00:01:49.534 --> 00:01:52.300
about a lot of his colleagues
in the Senate.

00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:55.133
And I checked in with his staff.

00:01:55.133 --> 00:01:56.968
I was like,
"How's everything going?"

00:01:56.968 --> 00:01:59.200
They were like, "He's skipping
the caucus lunches

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:01.267
for a little while.
-Yeah.

00:02:01.267 --> 00:02:02.734
-He's doing okay."

00:02:02.734 --> 00:02:04.734
I mean, he knew that
this was gonna make for,

00:02:04.734 --> 00:02:07.934
like, a weird few weeks
or months at work.

00:02:07.934 --> 00:02:11.100
But it's to his credit
that he went along with it.

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:13.234
-Well, you know,
it's called "A Reckoning,"

00:02:13.234 --> 00:02:15.534
and that's a very apt title
for this book.

00:02:15.534 --> 00:02:18.801
And even you talk about how he
was very careful with his words.

00:02:18.801 --> 00:02:22.067
It does feel like this moment

00:02:22.067 --> 00:02:25.467
has broken him to some degree
and it's broken his spirit.

00:02:25.467 --> 00:02:29.067
And he is using this
as a warning to all of us.

00:02:29.067 --> 00:02:32.734
-I think that's why he
ultimately decided to do this.

00:02:32.734 --> 00:02:36.400
I approached him just
a few weeks after January 6th,

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:39.400
and I still remember
our first meeting

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:42.167
was in his, like,
little Senate office.

00:02:42.167 --> 00:02:44.567
The barbed-wire fence
was still around the Capitol.

00:02:44.567 --> 00:02:47.701
And he --
Like, something snapped in him

00:02:47.701 --> 00:02:49.801
almost after January 6th

00:02:49.801 --> 00:02:54.934
because it demonstrated
just how far his party

00:02:54.934 --> 00:02:58.267
had kind of descended into what
he considers authoritarianism.

00:02:58.267 --> 00:03:00.367
He told me in one
of our first meetings,

00:03:00.367 --> 00:03:03.033
you know, "A very large portion
of my party

00:03:03.033 --> 00:03:05.267
really doesn't believe
in the Constitution."

00:03:05.267 --> 00:03:07.934
And I think that was
eye-opening for him.

00:03:07.934 --> 00:03:12.868
And he decided that it was time
to sort of tell the stories

00:03:12.868 --> 00:03:16.200
of what he had seen
inside these caucus rooms,

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:18.934
inside Republican politics
over the last 30 years

00:03:18.934 --> 00:03:21.434
so that Americans would know,

00:03:21.434 --> 00:03:22.968
you know,
just what we're up against,

00:03:22.968 --> 00:03:25.567
what's happening and what's
happening to his party.

00:03:25.567 --> 00:03:28.367
-There's a very real, obviously,
threat he's addressing.

00:03:28.367 --> 00:03:30.267
There's also, you know,
very funny ways

00:03:30.267 --> 00:03:32.400
he describes his colleagues.

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:34.534
Well, not that funny.

00:03:34.534 --> 00:03:38.434
Ted Cruz. "Scary,
demagogue, frightening."

00:03:38.434 --> 00:03:40.767
Mitch McConnell told Romney,
"You're lucky you can say

00:03:40.767 --> 00:03:42.234
the things we all think."

00:03:42.234 --> 00:03:43.767
That is one of
Romney's messages,

00:03:43.767 --> 00:03:45.934
is he isn't actually
the only one who thinks this.

00:03:45.934 --> 00:03:48.067
He's the only one
who says it out loud.

00:03:48.067 --> 00:03:50.367
J.D. Vance. "I don't know
that I can disrespect

00:03:50.367 --> 00:03:53.901
someone more than J.D. Vance.

00:03:53.901 --> 00:03:57.234
How do you sit
next to him at lunch?"

00:03:57.234 --> 00:04:00.634
-And now he's stuck
sitting next to him at lunch.

00:04:00.634 --> 00:04:02.367
-Yeah.
-I mean, you know, look.

00:04:02.367 --> 00:04:06.067
Like, when these quotes
started to come out,

00:04:06.067 --> 00:04:08.334
people were like, "Oh,
Mitt Romney is being petty,"

00:04:08.334 --> 00:04:10.234
or he's, you know,
consumed with these

00:04:10.234 --> 00:04:11.501
old resentments or whatever.

00:04:11.501 --> 00:04:13.434
But, like, I think it's really

00:04:13.434 --> 00:04:14.901
he's kind of heartbroken.

00:04:14.901 --> 00:04:17.000
Like, he believed in this party.

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:19.601
He believed in what he thought

00:04:19.601 --> 00:04:21.634
were the core tenets
of the Republican Party.

00:04:21.634 --> 00:04:23.400
And instead
what it turns out is,

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:25.968
like, all these guys
that he once respected

00:04:25.968 --> 00:04:27.501
just lined up behind

00:04:27.501 --> 00:04:30.868
this, like, manifestly crazy
person in Donald Trump.

00:04:30.868 --> 00:04:32.267
And now he, you know --

00:04:32.267 --> 00:04:34.033
And they're all telling him
in private,

00:04:34.033 --> 00:04:36.901
"We agree with you, Mitt, but
we can't say the same things

00:04:36.901 --> 00:04:38.534
because we have to
win re-election."

00:04:38.534 --> 00:04:42.501
And so I think that, you know,
he's well within his rights

00:04:42.501 --> 00:04:45.467
to say a few of these quotes
that we have in the book.

00:04:45.467 --> 00:04:47.734
-And it's also interesting
because this is --

00:04:47.734 --> 00:04:50.100
You know, you sort of have
documentary evidence.

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:51.667
This is not something
that he's doing now.

00:04:51.667 --> 00:04:53.467
These are things that
he has written in journals.

00:04:53.467 --> 00:04:55.501
These are things --
These are thoughts he's had...

00:04:55.501 --> 00:04:59.000
-Going back a decade or longer.
Yeah.

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:01.234
-Donald Trump --
it won't surprise anybody --

00:05:01.234 --> 00:05:03.434
was not a fan of the book.

00:05:03.434 --> 00:05:05.467
But I should note this is --
I want to just read

00:05:05.467 --> 00:05:09.834
the first couple lines
because this is a biography.

00:05:09.834 --> 00:05:11.601
-Correct.
-You wrote this book.

00:05:11.601 --> 00:05:13.200
-That's right.

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:15.567
-"Mitt Romney, a total loser
that only a mother could love,

00:05:15.567 --> 00:05:19.234
just wrote a book
which is much like him --

00:05:19.234 --> 00:05:21.133
boring, horrible,
and totally predictable."

00:05:21.133 --> 00:05:25.934
So...Donald Trump thinks
that Mitt Romney wrote a book.

00:05:25.934 --> 00:05:27.567
-He did. [ Laughs ]

00:05:27.567 --> 00:05:30.701
He seems slightly confused
between biography and memoir.

00:05:30.701 --> 00:05:32.033
-Yeah.
-In his defense,

00:05:32.033 --> 00:05:35.334
he probably has never read
one of either, so...

00:05:35.334 --> 00:05:37.000
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:38.200
-I think that's accurate.

00:05:38.200 --> 00:05:43.033
-I will say --
I will say I texted it --

00:05:43.033 --> 00:05:45.567
When I saw that,
I texted that to Mitt.

00:05:45.567 --> 00:05:48.767
And his response --
his response was,

00:05:48.767 --> 00:05:51.934
"Ha ha ha.
He's such a whack job."

00:05:51.934 --> 00:05:53.434
That's right.

00:05:53.434 --> 00:05:56.767
-It's so funny because, like,
Trump might think he wrote it

00:05:56.767 --> 00:05:58.334
because his face
is on the cover,

00:05:58.334 --> 00:06:02.501
but if that's true,
Trump wrote 100 books this year.

00:06:02.501 --> 00:06:06.968
Like, Donald Trump's on
the cover of every other book.

00:06:06.968 --> 00:06:09.534
You also -- It does seem like --

00:06:09.534 --> 00:06:11.100
Certainly as excerpts
coming out,

00:06:11.100 --> 00:06:13.133
a lot of the press
is talking about the moment

00:06:13.133 --> 00:06:15.367
we're living in now,
but in 2012,

00:06:15.367 --> 00:06:18.834
Romney obviously was the GOP
nominee running against Obama.

00:06:18.834 --> 00:06:21.000
And you discovered --
Not a thing you knew.

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:23.767
Not a thing people are paying
that much attention to now.

00:06:23.767 --> 00:06:25.868
That, in 2012,
six weeks before the election,

00:06:25.868 --> 00:06:27.901
he almost dropped out.
-Yeah. This is one of

00:06:27.901 --> 00:06:30.133
the most fascinating things
I found in his journals.

00:06:30.133 --> 00:06:32.901
Like, you know, it was after

00:06:32.901 --> 00:06:35.767
that hot-mic moment came out.

00:06:35.767 --> 00:06:38.667
Remember when he talked
about the 47% of Americans

00:06:38.667 --> 00:06:41.834
who would never vote for him
and he kind of disparaged them?

00:06:41.834 --> 00:06:44.033
It was in a closed-door
fundraiser.

00:06:44.033 --> 00:06:47.467
So, that tape came out,
and, you know, at the time,

00:06:47.467 --> 00:06:49.434
publicly,
he tried to defend himself

00:06:49.434 --> 00:06:52.033
and say the comments
are being taken out of context

00:06:52.033 --> 00:06:53.400
or whatever.

00:06:53.400 --> 00:06:57.133
In his journals, he was
anguished over this coming out

00:06:57.133 --> 00:07:00.334
to the point where people
in his circle thought he was,

00:07:00.334 --> 00:07:01.767
like, clinically depressed.

00:07:01.767 --> 00:07:03.901
He couldn't sleep.
he couldn't eat.

00:07:03.901 --> 00:07:05.734
He would every night
go into his journals

00:07:05.734 --> 00:07:08.701
and write like, "I'm so stupid.
I can't believe I did this."

00:07:08.701 --> 00:07:10.567
Really beating himself up.

00:07:10.567 --> 00:07:12.567
And he writes about
in his journals

00:07:12.567 --> 00:07:15.234
one night, six weeks
before the election,

00:07:15.234 --> 00:07:17.334
he called the chief strategist
of his campaign

00:07:17.334 --> 00:07:20.033
and said,
"I think I need to drop out.

00:07:20.033 --> 00:07:23.501
You should let Chris Christie
or somebody take take the job."

00:07:23.501 --> 00:07:26.234
That's kind of
how depressed he was.

00:07:26.234 --> 00:07:27.834
I think it also speaks to, like,

00:07:27.834 --> 00:07:29.801
there's something about
Mitt Romney where he, like,

00:07:29.801 --> 00:07:32.033
desperately hates
letting people down.

00:07:32.033 --> 00:07:34.801
And he felt like with that gaffe

00:07:34.801 --> 00:07:36.901
that was kind of
derailing his campaign,

00:07:36.901 --> 00:07:39.934
he had let down his, you know,
the people who supported him,

00:07:39.934 --> 00:07:41.901
the people
who had donated to him.

00:07:41.901 --> 00:07:44.133
You know, he eventually
pulled himself back up,

00:07:44.133 --> 00:07:46.000
and he lost the election,

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:49.234
but, you know, that moment,
I thought, was really revealing.

00:07:49.234 --> 00:07:50.868
-It must be a weird time for him

00:07:50.868 --> 00:07:52.734
because he is still
a conservative.

00:07:52.734 --> 00:07:54.801
He has conservative principles.

00:07:54.801 --> 00:07:58.734
He stands for a lot
of conservative ideals,

00:07:58.734 --> 00:08:02.834
and yet his party
has, you know, ostracized him.

00:08:02.834 --> 00:08:05.767
There are times where the left,
you know, eats it up

00:08:05.767 --> 00:08:07.834
when he says negative things
about his colleagues,

00:08:07.834 --> 00:08:12.234
but ultimately, he's no hero
of the left, either.

00:08:12.234 --> 00:08:16.501
One thing that I did find
endearing on a human level

00:08:16.501 --> 00:08:19.300
is he has connected
with President Biden

00:08:19.300 --> 00:08:22.100
on just the idea
of what it's like to age.

00:08:22.100 --> 00:08:25.300
-Yes, he has struck up
this friendship with Joe Biden

00:08:25.300 --> 00:08:27.601
the last couple of years
because he found that, like,

00:08:27.601 --> 00:08:30.701
they actually get along,
you know, on some things

00:08:30.701 --> 00:08:32.834
even though
they disagree on politics.

00:08:32.834 --> 00:08:34.734
And they spend
a lot of their time

00:08:34.734 --> 00:08:37.734
commiserating about
the indignities of getting old

00:08:37.734 --> 00:08:40.133
as two old guys are wont to do.

00:08:40.133 --> 00:08:44.267
But there's this one story
about, apparently,

00:08:44.267 --> 00:08:46.567
during one of these calls,
he told Joe Biden,

00:08:46.567 --> 00:08:49.100
"You know, I've been watching
some of your public events

00:08:49.100 --> 00:08:52.601
and you shuffle when you walk
and it makes you look old.

00:08:52.601 --> 00:08:55.133
And one of the tricks
that I've learned

00:08:55.133 --> 00:08:56.734
is take longer strides.

00:08:56.734 --> 00:08:59.200
It'll make you look youthful
and energetic."

00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:02.133
And so, apparently,
a few weeks later,

00:09:02.133 --> 00:09:03.601
Joe Biden called him back
and said,

00:09:03.601 --> 00:09:06.267
"Mitt, I took your advice
and it worked."

00:09:06.267 --> 00:09:08.200
[ Laughter ]

00:09:08.200 --> 00:09:10.501
He said, "I had given this --
I gave a speech,

00:09:10.501 --> 00:09:12.701
and I finished,
and I was leaving the stage,

00:09:12.701 --> 00:09:15.000
and I made a point
to take big, long steps.

00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:17.367
And I got off,
and the first lady was there,

00:09:17.367 --> 00:09:19.734
and she said,
'What's gotten into you?!'"

00:09:19.734 --> 00:09:22.234
[ Laughter ]

00:09:22.234 --> 00:09:24.133
-Well, he should write a book,

00:09:24.133 --> 00:09:27.067
like, "How to Walk
Impressively."

00:09:27.067 --> 00:09:29.133
Yeah, exactly.
Congratulations on this.

00:09:29.133 --> 00:09:31.133
Thanks so much
for being here tonight.

00:09:31.133 --> 00:09:33.567
I really enjoy talking to you.
McKay Coppins, everybody.

00:09:33.567 --> 00:09:35.133
"Romney: A Reckoning"
is out now.

00:09:35.133 --> 00:09:36.968
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

